# Line endings: LF everywhere, enforced.
#
# This is not a style preference. The scaffold is consumed by bash inside WSL and by the
# container build, and CRLF breaks it outright — a shebang or `set -euo pipefail` with a
# trailing \r fails to parse. This has already happened once: the copy of this scaffold in the
# Documentation repo was handing out a CRLF `new-sandbox.sh` on a fresh clone, because that
# repo is checked out with core.autocrlf=true on Windows.
#
# core.autocrlf=true is set globally on this machine, so without these rules the same bug
# arrives here on the next clone. The rules below win over that setting.
#
# `container/*.sh` are additionally \r-stripped by the Dockerfile, but `new-sandbox.sh` runs on
# the host via `bash new-sandbox.sh` and has no such safety net. It is the one that bites.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out for the load-bearing files, so the intent survives someone simplifying the rule
# above. VERIFY-ASSERTIONS.md S1 checks these for CR bytes.
*.sh              text eol=lf
Dockerfile        text eol=lf
dockerignore      text eol=lf
*.json            text eol=lf
*.md              text eol=lf

# PowerShell only ever runs on Windows, which is fine with LF; kept uniform so there is exactly
# one rule to remember rather than an exception to look up.
*.ps1             text eol=lf

# Never normalize these — binary or generated.
*.crt             binary
*.png             binary
*.tar.gz          binary
